<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oregon Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oregon Treaty was signed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 15 June 1846, ending their dispute over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-country">Oregon Country</a> in the Pacific Northwest. It extended the border along the 49th parallel from the Rocky Mountains to the coast, leaving all of Vancouver Island to Britain. The treaty settled a crisis that had raised fears of war, though the boundary through the San Juan Islands was not settled until 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty.jpg" alt="Oregon Treaty" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oregoncountry.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Treaty of 1818 set the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and British North America along the 49th parallel from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a> to the Rocky Mountains, and provided for joint control for ten years of the region to the west, which Americans called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-country">Oregon Country</a> and the British the Columbia Department. Joint control grew steadily less tolerable. A British minister rejected the offer of Presidents John Tyler and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-k-polk">James K. Polk</a> to settle at the 49th parallel, and American expansionists of the All Oregon movement called for annexing the region up to 54°40′ north. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a> began in April 1846 the Polk administration chose compromise, to avoid a war on two fronts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>On 12 June 1846 the Senate voted 38 to 12 to recommend that Polk accept British proposals to negotiate. The treaty was negotiated by the Secretary of State James Buchanan and the British envoy Richard Pakenham, and was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, on 15 June 1846. It ended the joint occupation and set the boundary at the 49th parallel, except that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept all of Vancouver Island, and it left Point Roberts as an exclave. Navigation of the channels and straits south of the parallel was to remain free to both parties, and the property rights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> and of British subjects south of the line were to be respected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> organized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> in 1848 and Washington Territory in 1853, while the British portion became the Colony of Vancouver Island in 1849 and, after the Fraser Canyon Gold Rush, the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> in 1858. Because the treaty placed the boundary in the middle of the channel without saying which, the ownership of the San Juan Islands was disputed, and a bloodless confrontation in 1859, the Pig War, had one casualty, a pig. In 1872 an arbitrator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, decided on 21 October in favour of the United States. The British press greeted the treaty with relief.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oregon Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Oregon Treaty</b> of 1846 left Britain only the lands north of the 49th parallel, the Crown made Vancouver Island a colony in 1849 and leased it to the Hudson's Bay Company on condition that it bring settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Colony of Vancouver Island</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Oregon Treaty</b> of 1846 gave the area south of the 49th parallel to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-oregon">Provisional Government of Oregon</a></li></ul></section>
